==English==
===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkʌni/}}

===Alternate forms===
: {{term|coni}}, {{term|cony}}, {{term|conny}}, {{term|coney}}<ref> In the sense of "rabbit; hyrax," as made in [[w:King James Version|King James Version]], a change was forced to pronounce {{term|coney}} as rhyming with {{term|boney}} instead of {{term|honey}} and {{term|money}}. </ref>

===Etymology 1===
Variant of {{term|cony}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|cunnies}}

# {{obsolete}} A [[cony]], [[rabbit]].
#*'''1611''', ''{{w|King James Version}}'', [[s:Bible (King James)/Proverbs#Chaper 30|Proverbs 30]]:26:
#*: The '''conies''' are but a feeble folk, yet make they their houses in the rocks;
#*'''1603''', {{w|John Florio}}, translating Michel de Montaigne, ''Essays'', II.12:
#*:What? shall Philosophie [...] make me like a fearfull '''cunnie''' creepe into some lurking-hole, and like a craven to tremble and yeeld?
#*'''1603''', {{w|John Florio}}, translating Michel de Montaigne, ''Essays'', II.8:
#*:I doe beware and keepe my selfe from such treasons, and '''cunny-catching''' in mine owne bosome, not by an unquiet, and tumultuary curiosity, but rather by a diversion and resolution.
#*'''c. 1591''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''The Taming of the Shrew'', First Folio 1623, IV.1:
#*:Come, you are so full of '''conicatching'''.

=====Synonyms=====
* [[bunny]]

=====Related terms=====
* [[cuniculus]] : "rabbit," originally meaning "burrow"
* [[cuniculture]] : [[w:Cuniculture|raising '''cunnies''']] in the [[warren]] or [[burrow]]s

===Etymology 2===
Diminutive form of {{term|cunt}} with {{term|-y}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|cunnies}}

# {{vulgar|slang}} A [[cunt]], [[vulva]].

=====Synonyms=====
# (''punning slang'') [[coney]] <ref>This synonymy holds only when {{term|coney}} is not an alternate form. </ref>
#* '''1622''': Philip Massinger: ''The Virgin-Martyr'', Act I, Scene 1
#*: A pox upon your Christian cockatrices! They cry, like poulterers' wives, 'No money, no coney.'

